From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexandre Derumier <aderumier@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 08:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] qasf/dmowrapper: Decommit the source allocators before taking
 the streaming lock.

The streaming thread holds stream_cs while it delivers output, and it can
block there in the downstream allocator's GetBuffer(): amstream's
DirectDraw stream waits until the application queues a sample. Stopping
the graph then deadlocked: dmo_wrapper_cleanup_stream() waited for
stream_cs before decommitting the allocators, the only thing that makes
GetBuffer() return.

Decommit first, as Windows' transform filters do when they go inactive,
then take the lock to flush the DMO. Burning Striker (Eolith) stops its
intro movie this way (IAMMultiMediaStream, AVI with a WMV decoder DMO): the
game hung on a black screen.
---
 dlls/qasf/dmowrapper.c | 6 +++++-
 1 file changed, 5 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/dlls/qasf/dmowrapper.c
+++ b/dlls/qasf/dmowrapper.c
@@ -950,13 +950,17 @@
 
     IUnknown_QueryInterface(filter->dmo, &IID_IMediaObject, (void **)&dmo);
 
-    EnterCriticalSection(&filter->filter.stream_cs);
+    /* Decommit the downstream allocators before taking the streaming lock: the
+     * streaming thread holds it while it waits in a downstream GetBuffer(),
+     * which only returns once its allocator is decommitted. */
     for (i = 0; i < filter->source_count; ++i)
     {
         if (filter->sources[i].pin.pin.peer)
             IMemAllocator_Decommit(filter->sources[i].pin.pAllocator);
     }
 
+    EnterCriticalSection(&filter->filter.stream_cs);
+
     IMediaObject_Flush(dmo);
     IMediaObject_FreeStreamingResources(dmo);
 
